<<~ ahu #/head>>

! Persona-Policy ~ one face out, the multitude within

The multi-persona identity core (persona-circle: a human as a private Circle of selves, each an all-hardened key at its own handle-index) ''carries three operator rulings'' that fix how a vessel wears its selves and how it recovers them. Each ruling ''bends toward the same law'': the mesh refuses a global identity-now, so it never builds the Single Identity Number even to defend itself. The rulings land 2026-07-20, dispatched to code at #64 stage 4.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/one-face>>

!! Ruling 1 ~ one face to the mesh, reboot to switch (PRIVATE-all, PUBLIC-one)

''A vessel shows the mesh ONE face at a time.'' The public/carry shore serves ''only the WORN persona''; the human's own private pool sees ''all N''. So the disclosure asymmetry reads: ''PRIVATE-all, PUBLIC-one'' — the multitude stays whole inside, one persona reaches outward.

''Switching a persona REBOOTS into the new identity'' — never a live hot-swap. The active-persona pointer ''reads at BOOT'': `wearPersona` sets it, the boot reads it, and the vessel re-stands AS that persona. This matches the built selector exactly (the mask you put on holds until you take it off and re-stand the house beneath). //"No two equals the same"// (Principia Discordia): each boot re-enacts a ''distinct identity-instance'', never a fused continuity across the switch.

The wall stays custody-by-TYPE: wearing a persona ''REQUIRES that this vessel HOLD its sovereign root'' — you sign only as a self whose secret you carry (persona-vault#wearPersona).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/cryptographic-individual>>

!! Ruling 2 ~ each PersonaGroup a cryptographic individual (Sybil-of-one, the accepted cost)

''You CANNOT pin "one human = one custodian" in crypto — that pinning IS the sin.'' A cryptographic layer that asserted "these N faces trace to one person" would rebuild the global identity-now the mesh forbids (the Nymwars root, persona-circle#the-vault). So the ruling ''declines the pin'': each ''PersonaGroup stands as its OWN cryptographic individual'', with its own root, its own device-delegation edge, and its own escrow (#/distinct-recovery).

''A human faking a cabal of their own PersonaGroups — a Sybil-of-one — reads as the ACCEPTED COST'' of the inversion-of-control. The crypto ''prices'' Sybil per-scope; it never eliminates it (persona-circle#honest-scope: scarcity lives outside the math). The answer runs ''SOCIAL, never cryptographic'': ''kahu as custodians, NOT rulers'' — vouch-and-price, exit-not-voice, legitimacy-held-in-talk-story (cabal-realm#the-unswept-corner; carry-contract#the-frame). The mesh ''holds the residual adversary in story tension'' rather than pretending a signature closes it.

The honest wall stays lit, dual to the wax-stamp's: ''"a valid key" reads as crypto fact; "one person behind these keys" does NOT'' — and the mesh ''wants it that way'', because the alternative wears N masks over a smuggled root.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-managed-persona>>

!! Ruling 2b ~ the MANAGED persona, and why the human stays unbound (operator ruling, 2026-09-04)

''A human `#has` multiple PersonaGroups, and WEARS a Persona on a device.'' The Persona carries identity here; the human enters as no object at all.

''A PERSONA MANAGES A PERSONAGROUP — never a device owner, and never a human.'' Management runs ''inside'' the persona layer, and that keeps the human unbound: no owner-of-record stands anywhere for a chain to climb to.

''THE MANAGER ROUTINELY DIFFERS FROM THE WEARER, and the design intends it.'' The canonical shape: //mom hands the kid the phone, and the kid wears "Mom's Kid" on that device// — where ''one of mom's Personas'' manages "Mom's Kid", never mom herself. PersonaGroups stand local-first and fleet-sync across the managing Persona's own device group.

⚠ ''SHARED DEVICES NAME NO EDGE CASE HERE — they give the model its shape.'' A design that bound one human to one device would have to call the majority of the world's households an exception. This one calls the managed persona ordinary and has nothing to except.

!!! Blind veils ~ Personas need not know about each other

''Personas can sometimes NOT EVEN KNOW ABOUT EACH OTHER.'' Each logged-in Persona carries a ''pet-name address-book device switcher'', and ''that Persona controls what appears in it''. So no Persona reads the multitude as a roster; it resolves into ''per-Persona views'', each authored by the Persona it belongs to.

⚠ ''This refines Ruling 1's "PRIVATE-all".'' The human may hold every thread — and ''the human never enters the system'', so no system-level all-N view stands to read, seize, or subpoena. ''A Persona sees what its own switcher shows and nothing behind the veil.'' Read together: PUBLIC-one, PERSONA-some, ''SYSTEM-none''.

The switcher ''applies the two-layer petname law'' (#/two-layer-petnames) to the multitude itself: the private map runs per-Persona, authored locally, never a public glamour.

!!! Why the model REFUSES the human rather than merely omitting them

''Ruling 2 declines to pin one-human-to-one-custodian because the pinning IS the sin.'' This ruling reads the same refusal forward: ''nothing binds the human to the system''. No human node, no human key, no human record stands — because ''binding a person creates the thing a hand can seize, correlate, or lose''.

⚠ ''The field converged on the same instinct and stopped one step short.'' Ink & Switch's Keyhive models //"a person (or more specifically **their authority**) as a group, with each of the persons devices being an individual member"// — the same refusal to name the human, expressed as ''a group of DEVICE KEYS''. This model keeps the layer they collapsed: ''a Persona holds a wearable role with a manager, never a bag of a device's keys'', and that lets the wearer differ from the manager. (`docs/pono/dyad-ladder-scout#the-convergent-shape` carries the field's reading and the challenge it puts back.)

⚠ ''A DEVICE OWNER NAMES THE NEAR MISS, and it reads identical until you ask what it binds.'' A reader reaches for "the owner sets up the personas", and an owner ''wears an infrastructure name over a human'' — naming one re-binds the human this model declines to bind, and hands any chain somewhere to climb. ''A Persona manages.'' The distance between the two words measures the distance between this model and every model carrying an owner-of-record.

!!! ⚠ The field's answer to this shape, and what it costs

A sweep of the measured abuse literature (`docs/pono/managed-identity-scout`) returns one finding aimed straight here:

> ''Nobody verifies who the manager is.'' Not one of forty supervised-identity products implemented multi-guardian consent; vendors assert //"only the device owner's consent is required"//; a regulator's own remedy accepts an unverified self-attestation checkbox.
>
> ⚠ //"A design where the manager is a persona with no human owner-of-record removes the last thing every existing system nominally relies on — an attributable person to attest."//

''The refusal to bind a human buys the privacy property and spends the field's only accountability anchor'' — an anchor that measured out as a checkbox nobody checked. So the question this ruling inherits reads: ''what stands in for attestation when no person stands behind the manager?'' The sweep found no system that answers it.

⚠ And it found ''no exit anywhere'': across every shipped supervised-identity system and every guardianship design, the subject's exit gates on the manager. The nearest thing standing is ''a petition right that cannot be confiscated'', never an exit — while the primitive that WOULD work (guardians who recover but never transact, removable unilaterally after a timelock) ''has never been pointed at a manager→subject relation''.

''What stands open'': how management TRANSFERS, whether a managed wearer can ever LEAVE with their Persona, what a blind veil costs when a managing Persona goes dark, and the abuse surface the manager relation opens — priced nowhere yet, against a literature that has already measured the household's authorized principal as its adversary.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/distinct-recovery>>

!! Ruling 3 ~ distinct recovery custodians per persona

''Each persona recovers through its OWN custodians.'' Shared custodians across personas would ''correlate them'' — a custodian holding shares for both Guru-Josh and Telarus-KSC learns they name one human, and web2's global-now walks back in through the recovery door. So the recovery split runs ''PER persona-root'' (persona-vault: a persona-1 quorum reconstructs persona-1's root, never persona-0's), and the ''custodian sets stay disjoint'' by ruling. Recovery re-admits a device; it never re-fuses the selves.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/two-layer-petnames>>

!! The Two-Layer Pet-Names ~ private label ⊥ public glamour (Zooko)

A persona carries ''two names beside its key'', and the key stays the only global one. ''Zooko's triangle resolves'' the same way handle-card proves it: the ''GLOBAL name STAYS the pubkey'' — the veiled-user key the persona-HD tree derives — secure-and-decentralised because it IS the key; ''memorability rides a LOCAL name'':

* ''The OWN side ~ the private pet-name map'' (`{handleIndex -> petname}`, persona-petname). The human's own label for their own faces — "work", "the-guru", "my-burner" — ''freely renamable'' and ''never PUBLICLY federated''. It ''RIDES the human's own private fleet'': the map private-federates over the ''@persona bag'' across their own vessel-pool (the fleet ride, below). The wall the shore holds runs ''PUBLIC, not cross-vessel'': no board write exists in it. A ''multitude-view'' reads the whole self across the vessel — `{persona → pet-name, held-here?, has-public-handle?}` — so the human sees every face while the mesh sees one.
* ''The OTHER side ~ the handle-book'' (handle-book). The recogniser's local labels for ''OTHERS'' nyms' — never on any wire.
* ''The ONLY name that federates PUBLICLY ~ the public glamour'' (persona-glamour). The persona's veiled key ''signs a HandleCard'' whose `glamour` shows a chosen display (never a legal identity); it ''announces onto the per-Nexus @crossroads WHO board'' (who-face), self-certifying, monotone-versioned. ''What supersedes it is a higher version, never a clock'' — a reader on any island orders two cards by their own monotone version without consulting a shared now; a local clock may hint that a card has gone quiet, and a hint never carries a verdict two islands must agree on.

!!! The Binding Law

> ''Only a PUBLICLY ANNOUNCED HANDLE binds a PersonaGroup to a public glamour.''

The announce IS the binding act — a persona's veiled key signs its own HandleCard and puts it on the board. ''No other path may carry a persona's name onto a federating surface.'' A pet-name string that reaches a published document publishes a face the human never announced, and the string's own harmlessness never licenses it: the ''declaring act'' makes a name public, never the characters in it. The two MAY read word-for-word identical — the operator's `Telarus, KSC` names a compartment privately AND announces publicly — and they stay ''two acts with two owners'' (declaration ⊥ measurement, identity-enacts-relation). ''A private label reaching a board reads as a leak, never a shortcut.''

!!! The announce IS a kūkala ~ so the board's two authorities govern it

A HandleCard announce lands on the crossroads as a ''kūkala'' — a public cry pinned to a board that only grows — and it inherits that board's whole grammar rather than needing a rule of its own:

* the announce reads as a ''notice'': this persona cries this Handle here.
* a withdrawal reads as a ''tombstone'' the persona signs over its OWN cry. Taking a face down is as public as putting it up, because a board where things vanish quietly cannot be read — and a tombstone over ANOTHER persona's card does nothing at all, which is what keeps every member from holding a delete button over the board.
* a Nexus refusal reads as a ''kāpae'', shadowing the SUBJECT rather than one cry, ''remove-wins'', before or after. Two islands with no shared now cannot agree which act came first; if a later cry could outlive a refusal, anyone refused at the Gate re-pins from a partition and stands again, and the Gate becomes a request.

So the fold answers //what stands// order-independently, and the ''two authorities never blur'': a persona governs only its own cries, and the Nexus governs the board. The declaring-act law rides underneath both — the cry makes the name public, never the characters in it.

!!! The declaration ~ the store that keeps the other two apart

Between the private label and the published record sits ''a name the human MEANS to wear and has not yet announced''. Reading that intent off the pet-name would ''weld the two registers'': a human whose compartment reads `wyrm-burner` could then never stand as `The Lindwyrm`, and every private label would quietly become a public commitment. So the intent takes ''its own store and its own act'' (persona-declare): `--handle` declares what a persona answers to outward, and `--seat` stands it for a Kahu chair on this node. ''Neither implies the other'' — a persona MAY declare a Handle and offer no chair (the ordinary case), and a seat claim carrying no Handle ''answers to no chair'' and never joins a roster.

''The seal joins on the DECLARED HANDLE, never on the private label.'' A chair belongs to whoever declared the name it bears AND stood for it; the pet-name reaches no roster and no document. That reads as the same law one layer down — ''the declaring act, never the characters, makes a name public''.

!!! The fleet ride ~ what travels between a human's own vessels

''A label a human sets on one vessel and loses on the next names the compartment badly.'' So the two NAMES ride the ''@persona bag'' — the PersonaGroup's own private plane, which the self-slot ''FLEET-syncs same-operator'' and the DeterministicFederationGate never volunteers to a cross-operator. One multitude, every device of the one human, no stranger. The verbs run `persona-label` · `persona-handle` · `persona-selves`, and ''no board shore stands reachable from them'' — the wall runs structural, as it does for the follow-graph one plane over.

''The SEAT claim does NOT ride.'' A Kahu chair names a seat on a ''PARTICULAR node'', so syncing it would let one device seat a persona on another device's seal. ''The fleet carries what a persona IS called; each node keeps what it stands for HERE.''

''The per-field stamp refuses the clobber.'' Each name carries its own value+stamp pair (`petname` beside `petname@`), so two vessels renaming ''different'' names — or different personas — touch disjoint keys and the field-merge keeps both. Where two renamed the ''same'' field, the later stamp reads; the stamp orders concurrent edits and ''settles nothing else'' (no shared now rides between a human's own islands), so a human overrules it by renaming again.

''Local-first, never daemon-required.'' A vessel FOUNDS before it breathes, so every write lands on the local floor first and mirrors up when the sock answers; a quiet sock reports ''NODE-LOCAL, never failure'', and `lares persona sync` carries the names up once the hearth stands.

''Four stores, never fused'': private-own (pet-name) ⊥ declared-own (Handle + seat) ⊥ public-own (glamour record) ⊥ others (handle-book). The pet-name map ''wants to sync'' across the human's own PersonaGroup vessel-pool — private-federated over a PRIVATE BAG — but that ride waits on the ''device-fleet'' (not yet built; blocks on Beelay). So the shore stands ''LOCAL-FIRST'', shaped so the cross-pool sync drops into the same store shape when the fleet lands.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/no-global-now-for-identity>>

!! The Deep Frame ~ no global-now for identity

The three rulings ''share one root'': the causal-islands law (#causal-islands: no global present, each island its own partial log) ''extended to IDENTITY''. There sits ''no global now for who-someone-is'' — no mesh-wide instant of "this human = these faces = one custodian". Every attempt to assert it manufactures the very global-now the substrate forbids (the same move carry-contract's immune system refuses for revocation, one plane over).

So the mesh ''declines to build the SIN even to defend itself''. It could bind personas to one custodian and catch the Sybil-of-one — and it ''MUST NOT'', because that catch IS the sin it refuses. The residual adversary (a human forging a cabal of their own selves) stays ''bounded where a mythos wants it'': answered by ''kahu as custodians not rulers'' (vouch, price, watch, exit), by ''fork-as-exit'' (cabal-realm: leave the captured realm, re-point your own petname), and by ''legitimacy-held-in-talk-story'' — never by a signature claiming a global identity-now. //Shadowland's refusal made law//: the net heals faster than the corps can burn a runner, and it never installs the corps' own registry to do it.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/edges>>

